    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm a bit confused by your analogies. Could you stick to the technical aspects of the classification systems? You mentioned the Dewey Decimal System struggles with \"chaotic\" nature—can you explain exactly how that manifests in terms of shelf-list maintenance and hierarchical rigidity specifically when dealing with irregular manuscript formats?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Dewey Decimal System (DDC) manifests its struggle with irregular manuscript formats through its rigid decimal hierarchy, which forces non-standard documents into pre-defined numerical slots that often lack the granularity required for unique 19th-century artifacts. When dealing with irregular formats, such as mixed-language correspondence or hybrid manuscript types, the DDC's fixed structure can lead to \"shelf-list maintenance\" nightmares where documents must be physically moved or assigned arbitrary, misleading call numbers to fit the system. This rigidity creates bottlenecks because the DDC lacks the alphanumeric flexibility to create specific, granular subdivisions for niche historical contexts without resorting to complex, unwieldy decimal extensions that are difficult for archivists to navigate.\n\nIn contrast, the Library of Congress Classification (LC) utilizes an alphanumeric system that allows for the creation of specific, granular call numbers tailored to unique historical documents. This flexibility means that as you acquire more non-standard manuscripts, you can expand the classification without disrupting the existing structure, effectively scaling metadata to accommodate the chaotic nature of the collection without forcing it into a rigid mold. While the DDC might require constant re-shelving to maintain order amidst irregularities, the LC system offers a stable, scalable framework that grows organically with",
      "source": "target",
